 i  couldn't use that i planned to and  that's fine that actually gave me an  opportunity to fix them communicating  that right away means that by the time  we're ready to build that model we'll  have that data and that will be awesome  whereas if we waited until the end of  this project then we would have to wait  even longer for that data to trickle in  eda insights as i was working through  some of the hypotheses i would end up  going back and changing hypotheses to  come because i would find new insights  that informed  whether those analyses would continue to  be valuable  and then specific business  recommendations we're going to walk  through an example of one of these  hypothesis explorations and it's around  the length of our trial so we went from  a 30-day trial to a seven-day trial as a  result of one of my analyses and that  specific business recommendation had big  impact during the time period that i was  working on this and informs my work  going down the pipe and so continuously  delivering insights gives more  opportunities for feedback and results  in a better analysis i highly recommend  it  so the way that you can do that is to  build deliverable assets as you learn  the interview transcripts and summaries  we've talked about  one key piece here for me has been  taking eda insights and packaging them  into something that i can share with  other people so that that knowledge  doesn't just end with me  and then a new data set for machine  learning so that's kind of the end goal  here but really we're developing all of  the understanding and the insight around  this data before we get to that  so one thing that you can include in an  eda deliverable is visualizations i love  klaus wilkie's book the fundamentals of  data visualization can't recommend it  enough it's free online and he also has  code available that you can use to make  your own visualizations so just the way  that jesse mazda pack will share code on  twitter  to get you started with the database and  then you make it a lot prettier i've  taken some of the code that klaus wilkie  has shared and tuned it to my data in  order to share this stuff with  stakeholders and come up with some  really pretty  impactful data visualizations that help  people understand the relationships that  i'm uncovering and so this is one  example of a visualization that we  looked at  for people who used a specific product  that we have are they doing more or less  on the site and then we know when we're  ready to build a model like maybe we  want to um get some variables around  that also for product this is super  useful because then they know we need to  push people to this part of the product  this actually seems to make a difference  so there's lots of ways that those  insights could be useful to other people  beyond the model  another thing that we came up with is  the idea of this analysis summary card  and so  you can see on the left we have our  little logo for the dagger group so it's  very branded it's very clean and  polished and that's really important to  me so as i'm sharing results with other  people i want them to feel like we've  put a lot of effort into them they're  very clean and these are artifacts that  are going to live beyond this analysis  right i'm saving all of the work that  i'm doing so that people will understand  what analysis has been done how they can  apply it and what we've learned from it  and so here's an example of that  analysis summary card this is just  mocked up by our design team  see  um but this is an actual analysis  summary card that i shared  and so i looked into this hypothesis of  trial users are active for more than the  first week of the trial  and built this visualization to see  whether they're using different  modalities on our site and when i have  outcomes here i have key insights here  this is just one like  bite size deliverable that i can create  so people can understand the analysis  that i did the outcomes and get a visual  really quickly  and then i take this and i make a long  form write up  so i want people to be able to reference  what was my methodology who did i look  at where are these insights applicable  so i clipped some pieces here  i included things like key insights to  really call out the things that we are  learning as a result of this analysis  i'm also including a big business  questions and my recommendations vary  directly so in this case like the data  supported shortening the length of our  trial i had quantitative evidence for  that  as a bonus i have qualitative evidence  because of all of the interviews that i  did this is where this idea came up and  so i'm able to take all of that data  combine it and make better decisions as  a business and also still be prepping  for that predictive model  and then the final piece here is  distributing your findings and so once  you have them you want to make sure that  you're sharing them with other people  so we have a channel for this project  and whenever i finish part of it i go  ahead and just upload it to the channel  and people are able to read the long  form see the quick  one-shot  summary and then go from there and  that's been really helpful  making tailored recommendations for  impact has been something that's gotten  a lot of value out of this project  so i thought that we should change the  length of the trial and then the impact  you can see directly is this uh seven  day trial project so we kicked off  another project to take the insights and  just go with those build with those  um and so being able to quantify the  impact to trace it over time has been  really helpful and all of this again is  just kind of bonus more bang for the  buck that we're getting as we're on our  way to build a model and use this data  that we haven't used before  so when it comes to getting buy-in i'm  going to go pretty quickly through this  section i know i have like a minute um  starts with an idea  this didn't come from someone telling me  hey go solve churn this actually came  because i saw that there were users  using our product  who um were paying us or who were paying  us and not using it and that seemed wild  and so um i took that idea thought more  about it eventually thought it'd be  worthwhile weighing out the opportunity  cost of those ideas is very important  you want to make sure that you're  spending your time on the most important  things  getting feedback from other people  seeing again where this could help this  is building momentum for your project  if you can quantify impact i talk with  finance to see if we made you know a 10  change here how much does that mean in  terms of dollars and that makes it  easier to present  and then finally coming up with a  project proposal  these are the pieces of my project  proposal so it has like an overview  pre-work or research that i need to do  the hypotheses i want to explore  deliverables how long i think it will  take and a pre-mortem things that i  think could go wrong how would this  project fail all of this makes me super  prepared to get buy-in for this project  and then also when i'm ready to get  started all the pre-work is done i know  what kind of analysis i need to be doing  i know how to structure it i know who  cares about what it just makes things a  lot easier to get these kinds of  projects off the ground  thank you so much for having me  thank you very much that was wonderful  so before we get into our next speaker
 

